WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.780
I want you to think just for a second about the systems in your life that you consider to be, you know, the most stable, like the ones you rely on completely.

00:09.180 --> 00:10.520
Right. Stuff you don't even question.

00:10.700 --> 00:17.720
Exactly. Maybe it's a hyper-optimized morning routine that gets you out the door in exactly 22 minutes.

00:18.100 --> 00:25.720
Or maybe it's a finely tuned monthly budget where literally every single dollar is assigned a specific job.

00:26.220 --> 00:28.940
Yeah. Or even something bigger like the global supply chain.

00:28.940 --> 00:39.520
Oh, totally. A supply chain that somehow manages to get a really specific electronics charger from a factory like halfway across the world to your front porch in under 12 hours.

00:40.080 --> 00:48.800
I mean, we tend to view these highly efficient, completely unchanging, deeply predictable structures as the absolute pinnacle of survival and success.

00:48.800 --> 00:49.480
We really do.

00:49.560 --> 00:51.120
Yeah. If something doesn't wobble, we think it's strong.

00:51.740 --> 00:55.540
But what if I told you to reconsider everything you know about stability?

00:55.540 --> 01:03.840
Like, what if the most efficient, optimized, rigid systems in your life are actually the ones closest to a sudden catastrophic collapse?

01:04.120 --> 01:07.740
I mean, it feels like an inherently contradictory idea, right?

01:07.820 --> 01:14.660
We are so deeply conditioned, really from childhood, to believe that stability is synonymous with resisting change.

01:14.800 --> 01:16.960
Yeah. We equate stillness with strength.

01:16.960 --> 01:25.820
Exactly. Like, if a bridge sways too much, we think it's poorly built. Or if a company changes its strategy every quarter, we think it's failing.

01:26.140 --> 01:37.420
But today, we are throwing that entire worldview completely out the window. We are doing a deep dive into this brilliant, frankly, just mind-bending 2026 paper.

01:37.520 --> 01:38.540
It really is incredible.

01:38.540 --> 01:46.140
It's titled, The Geometry of Rewiring, Ecological Networks, Admissible Trajectories, and the Conservation of Transformability.

01:46.340 --> 01:49.320
And it's authored by an independent researcher who goes by the name Fliction.

01:49.580 --> 01:50.820
Which is quite a title, by the way.

01:50.820 --> 01:52.040
Oh, it's a mouthful for sure.

01:52.200 --> 01:52.320
Yeah.

01:52.420 --> 01:58.100
But our mission today is to explore a radically new framework for understanding resilience.

01:58.800 --> 02:04.920
We are going to completely deconstruct that deeply held idea that surviving means staying the same.

02:04.980 --> 02:05.180
Right.

02:05.180 --> 02:21.960
Instead, we're going to look at the actual math and the science, showing how, you know, ecosystems, human brains, global supply chains, and even artificial intelligence, how they only manage to survive by constantly, actively changing their fundamental underlying architecture.

02:22.400 --> 02:25.840
And I think the scope of Fliction's paper really is staggering.

02:25.840 --> 02:34.020
I mean, it begins grounded in physical ecology, literally looking at how forests, tropical soils, and lakes survive massive disruptions.

02:34.020 --> 02:41.140
But the underlying mathematics it introduces, they rapidly expand into this universal theory of survival.

02:41.480 --> 02:41.500
Yeah.

02:41.600 --> 02:46.640
It gives us a language to understand essentially anything that needs to persist in an unpredictable world.

02:46.740 --> 02:47.100
It does.

02:47.240 --> 02:47.860
It really does.

02:47.940 --> 02:48.080
Okay.

02:48.120 --> 02:53.300
Let's unpack this by looking at the trap we have been stuck in for, well, basically the last century.

02:53.460 --> 02:58.420
The paper argues that we've been clinging to something called the fixed-point equilibrium model of ecology.

02:58.420 --> 03:06.860
And when I was reading this section, it felt like, you know, early 20th century scientists were looking at a living, breathing forest and trying to treat it like a car engine.

03:07.180 --> 03:10.700
That is actually a very accurate read on the historical context.

03:10.880 --> 03:19.440
Because to understand why this fixed-point equilibrium model gained so much traction, you really have to look at the dominant mindset of the 20th century.

03:19.560 --> 03:20.820
Which was all about industry, right?

03:20.820 --> 03:21.300
Exactly.

03:21.660 --> 03:24.180
This was the era of rapid industrialization.

03:24.600 --> 03:29.720
It was the era of the assembly line, of mass production, and, you know, scientific management.

03:30.100 --> 03:35.500
The ultimate goals of human society at that time were optimization and absolute control.

03:35.720 --> 03:35.940
Right.

03:35.940 --> 03:42.900
So, when managers and early ecologists looked at the natural world, they just projected their own industrial desires onto it.

03:43.060 --> 03:48.680
They started viewing complex biological systems as if they were just complicated static machines.

03:48.900 --> 03:48.980
Yeah.

03:49.040 --> 03:52.840
They wanted nature to run on a predictable timetable, like a train schedule.

03:53.000 --> 03:53.720
They really did.

03:53.840 --> 03:59.980
And in their mathematical models, they defined stability using a very specific, rigid concept.

03:59.980 --> 04:09.460
The paper points out that this old worldview essentially relied on an equation where the state of the system tomorrow must be virtually identical to the state of the system today.

04:09.640 --> 04:09.860
Okay.

04:10.000 --> 04:11.780
So, in plain English, that means what?

04:11.900 --> 04:17.360
Well, say you walk into a forest today and you count exactly 100 wolves in 1,000 deer.

04:17.900 --> 04:24.100
A stable forest, under this old model, is one that has that exact same ratio next year and the decade after that.

04:24.740 --> 04:25.820
And a century from now.

04:25.820 --> 04:26.340
Oh, wow.

04:26.340 --> 04:38.000
So, if you go back a decade later and there are only 50 wolves, but now there are, I don't know, 200 coyotes and the deer population has shifted its grazing patterns, that is viewed as a total failure of the system.

04:38.280 --> 04:38.880
Precisely.

04:39.420 --> 04:45.620
Any deviation from what those managers called the historical baseline was immediately interpreted as degradation.

04:46.100 --> 04:47.920
It was just seen as disorder.

04:47.920 --> 04:48.520
Interesting.

04:48.720 --> 04:55.900
And this industrial mindset really birthed that popular metaphor of the ecosystem as a delicate house of cards.

04:56.700 --> 05:01.160
The assumption was that nature is this incredibly fragile, perfectly balanced tower.

05:01.480 --> 05:10.640
And if you pull even one card out, like if one species disappears or one energy pathway changes, the entire structure inevitably comes crashing down.

05:10.700 --> 05:14.020
Because every single node in the network was viewed as completely indispensable.

05:14.020 --> 05:14.360
Exactly.

05:14.780 --> 05:17.180
Indispensable to the structural integrity of the whole thing.

05:17.180 --> 05:21.340
You know, I kept thinking of a museum diorama while reading that part of the text.

05:21.420 --> 05:22.460
Oh, that's a good comparison.

05:22.760 --> 05:22.920
Yeah.

05:22.980 --> 05:26.400
You know those massive, beautiful displays in natural history museums?

05:26.580 --> 05:26.800
Mm.

05:26.900 --> 05:32.240
Where you have a taxidermy lion chasing a gazelle across a painted savanna frozen behind glass?

05:32.380 --> 05:32.660
Right.

05:32.780 --> 05:33.040
Yeah.

05:33.280 --> 05:41.260
It's visually stunning, but the entire purpose of the diorama is to freeze a single perfect moment in time forever.

05:41.260 --> 05:48.260
I mean, if the gazelle miraculously moved two inches to the left, the exhibit would be considered ruined, right?

05:48.400 --> 05:48.580
Yeah.

05:48.580 --> 05:49.680
Because it lost its original state.

05:49.780 --> 05:50.080
Totally.

05:50.220 --> 05:59.240
But what Fliction is arguing, what this entire new geometric view of ecology proves, is that real survival is nothing like a diorama.

05:59.240 --> 06:02.540
It is much more like an improvisational jazz band.

06:02.680 --> 06:11.460
Well, and the jazz band analogy gets you part of the way there because it captures the spontaneity, but it almost implies a conscious artistic intent to create a beautiful song.

06:11.580 --> 06:11.980
Oh, sure.

06:12.080 --> 06:12.240
Yeah.

06:12.240 --> 06:22.060
A more mechanical way to look at it, which aligns better with Fliction's physical evidence, is that real ecosystems are relentlessly, intensely dynamic on a physical level.

06:22.620 --> 06:32.200
The paper gathers empirical studies across terrestrial forests, freshwater lakes, deep marine systems, and the data completely shatters that museum diorama model.

06:32.520 --> 06:33.660
They just do not sit still.

06:33.840 --> 06:34.360
They don't.

06:34.360 --> 06:43.400
When environmental conditions change, say a severe drought hits or a wildfire sweeps through, species do not just sit there and accept their fate.

06:43.780 --> 06:45.700
They actively alter their diets.

06:46.080 --> 06:49.080
They physically migrate to entirely new altitudes.

06:49.620 --> 06:53.840
They shift their seasonal behaviors, altering when they mate or when they forage.

06:53.980 --> 06:57.160
So they aren't trying to return to the preferred state of the diorama at all.

06:57.520 --> 07:01.620
They are surviving through structural transformation rather than by resisting it.

07:01.700 --> 07:02.100
Exactly.

07:02.100 --> 07:07.880
And the critical distinction the author draws here is between the continuity of function and the continuity of structure.

07:08.520 --> 07:11.500
Fixed-point ecology completely conflated the two.

07:11.640 --> 07:12.620
Wait, explain that difference.

07:12.740 --> 07:13.860
Function versus structure.

07:14.140 --> 07:14.380
Sure.

07:14.520 --> 07:25.460
So the old model assumed that to maintain the core functions of an ecosystem like carbon cycling, water filtration, or the transfer of energy from sunlight to plants to animals,

07:25.460 --> 07:32.140
you had to maintain the exact same physical structure, the exact same species in the exact same numbers.

07:32.140 --> 07:32.420
Right.

07:32.620 --> 07:42.020
But ecosystems routinely preserved their broad functional processes while undergoing massive, almost unrecognizable structural transformations.

07:42.300 --> 07:44.380
Which raises a really fundamental question, honestly.

07:44.380 --> 07:52.180
If a forest or a lake isn't actually trying to return to some preferred historical state, does a healthy baseline even exist?

07:52.680 --> 07:56.960
Or have we just been measuring the health of nature with the completely wrong ruler for a hundred years?

07:57.320 --> 08:01.540
The papal makes a very compelling case that we have been using the wrong ruler entirely.

08:01.800 --> 08:04.240
We've been measuring stability by looking for immobility.

08:04.240 --> 08:08.100
We put a GPS tracker on a system and we celebrate if it hasn't moved.

08:08.740 --> 08:11.660
But the new metric Flixion proposes is navigability.

08:12.280 --> 08:16.420
The relevant question to ask a complex system is not, did you stay the same?

08:16.600 --> 08:21.280
The question is, do you still preserve a path forward through which you can keep functioning?

08:21.800 --> 08:25.920
The shift is moving from asking, are we still standing on the same spot?

08:26.060 --> 08:29.400
To asking, do we still have a trajectory out of this mess?

08:29.720 --> 08:30.900
That makes so much sense.

08:30.900 --> 08:38.500
But if ecosystems aren't just frozen statues, how exactly do they orchestrate this constant improvisation?

08:38.740 --> 08:41.060
Like, what are the mechanics of this rewiring?

08:41.260 --> 08:45.140
The paper introduces this through something it calls the RSVP framework.

08:45.260 --> 08:47.700
Right, the relativistic scalar vector plenum.

08:47.820 --> 08:50.200
It is a dense piece of terminology, I know.

08:50.420 --> 08:55.060
But it provides a gorgeous, highly accurate way to visualize physical reality.

08:55.060 --> 09:00.300
Yeah, I have to admit, when I first read the phrase localized constraint bundles within an evolving manifold,

09:00.300 --> 09:02.460
my brain briefly short-circuited.

09:02.580 --> 09:04.380
It's intimidating at first glance, for sure.

09:04.500 --> 09:09.280
But the text is essentially saying we need to stop looking at a species like a wolf or an oak tree,

09:09.760 --> 09:12.800
as an isolated object floating around in an empty void.

09:13.240 --> 09:13.600
Yes.

09:14.040 --> 09:16.940
Let's break that terminology down into its physical realities.

09:17.300 --> 09:19.900
In this framework, the ecosystem is a plenum.

09:20.220 --> 09:22.920
A plenum is simply a space that is completely full.

09:23.460 --> 09:23.740
Right.

09:23.940 --> 09:24.700
No empty space.

09:24.700 --> 09:25.180
Exactly.

09:25.420 --> 09:26.840
There is no empty void.

09:27.520 --> 09:32.960
It is a fabric entirely saturated with dynamic flows, energetic gradients, and interaction fields.

09:33.160 --> 09:33.360
Oh.

09:33.560 --> 09:35.940
And within this full fabric, you have two things.

09:36.120 --> 09:40.040
You have scalars, which are quantities you can measure at a specific point.

09:40.220 --> 09:40.640
Like what?

09:40.860 --> 09:46.660
Like the sheer mass of biological material, the density of a population, or the temperature of the soil,

09:46.740 --> 09:47.840
just amounts of things.

09:47.880 --> 09:50.720
And then you have vectors, which are the directional forces.

09:50.920 --> 09:51.880
Okay, so like movement.

09:51.880 --> 09:56.520
Yeah, the physical movement of an animal, the flow of calories from prey to predator,

09:56.860 --> 09:58.940
the direction of wind-carrying seeds.

09:59.240 --> 10:03.380
Okay, so under this framework, an oak tree isn't just a physical wooden object.

10:03.620 --> 10:06.720
It's a localized intersection of these scalars and vectors.

10:07.460 --> 10:14.420
It is a literal bottleneck or a constraint that dictates how water moves from the ground to the sky,

10:14.640 --> 10:19.640
how sunlight is captured and turned into sugar, and how squirrels allocate their energy foraging.

10:19.640 --> 10:21.900
That is the perfect way to visualize it.

10:22.260 --> 10:26.260
The oak tree is a bundle of constraints shaping the energetic fabric around it.

10:26.460 --> 10:29.260
And this brings us to the concept of rewiring.

10:29.620 --> 10:30.800
Right, the core of the paper.

10:30.900 --> 10:31.300
Exactly.

10:31.700 --> 10:36.400
In the old model, rewiring just meant a bird decided to eat a moth instead of a beetle.

10:37.300 --> 10:41.960
But fliction redefines rewiring as a distributed constraint computation.

10:42.420 --> 10:43.340
Okay, stop right there.

10:43.660 --> 10:46.560
You're making a forest sound like a silicon microchip.

10:46.560 --> 10:52.980
I really need to push back on this terminology, because calling it computation implies a level

10:52.980 --> 10:56.080
of intelligence or, like, systemic intent.

10:56.500 --> 11:01.220
If a starving wolf can't find a deer and out of sheer desperation starts eating rabbits,

11:01.740 --> 11:02.740
how is that a computation?

11:03.400 --> 11:07.700
It feels like we are elevating biological desperation to the level of algorithmic genius.

11:08.060 --> 11:13.100
That skepticism is completely warranted, and the author actually anticipates this exact pushback

11:13.100 --> 11:13.680
in the text.

11:14.280 --> 11:17.940
Fliction explicitly clarifies that we are not talking about conscious intent here.

11:18.020 --> 11:18.240
Okay.

11:18.240 --> 11:20.200
These are not random, desperate flails.

11:20.580 --> 11:22.580
They are constraint-sensitive redistributions.

11:22.960 --> 11:28.100
To understand why it is literally a computation, look at how we train artificial neural networks.

11:28.220 --> 11:29.200
Like AI models.

11:29.260 --> 11:29.540
Yes.

11:30.120 --> 11:35.400
When an AI model is learning to recognize a picture of a cat, it adjusts the weights of the

11:35.400 --> 11:37.960
millions of connections between its artificial neurons.

11:37.960 --> 11:42.400
It is searching for a mathematical solution that minimizes its error rate.

11:42.680 --> 11:48.140
The ecosystem is doing something mathematically identical, just with biology instead of code.

11:48.420 --> 11:48.620
Oh.

11:49.080 --> 11:52.600
So the interactions between the species are like the weights of the neural network.

11:52.760 --> 11:53.700
Yes, exactly.

11:53.940 --> 11:58.300
The strength of the interaction, how often the wolf hunts the rabbit, how much water the

11:58.300 --> 11:59.960
oak tree draws, those are their weights.

12:00.400 --> 12:04.360
When the environment shifts dramatically, the constraints in the plenum change.

12:04.360 --> 12:10.120
The system does not have a central brain analyzing the drought and issuing orders to the wildlife.

12:10.740 --> 12:13.780
No single animal has a master blueprint of the forest.

12:14.440 --> 12:20.120
Instead, the ecosystem actively, continuously re-weights those biological interactions across

12:20.120 --> 12:22.060
whatever pathways are physically available.

12:22.360 --> 12:22.640
Wow.

12:22.840 --> 12:27.660
It is computing a solution to the problem of survival in real time, physically, through

12:27.660 --> 12:29.460
the lived behaviors of the organisms.

12:30.100 --> 12:33.680
It is searching for what the author calls the manifold of admissible futures.

12:33.680 --> 12:36.680
The geometric space where survival is still possible.

12:37.060 --> 12:38.440
Okay, I think I grasped this now.

12:39.140 --> 12:45.700
The ecosystem is computing its own survival by constantly rerouting the flow of life through

12:45.700 --> 12:47.340
whatever pathways are available.

12:47.960 --> 12:52.340
It honestly reminds me of the organic flow of traffic in a really dense city.

12:52.480 --> 12:53.680
Oh, tell me more about that.

12:53.940 --> 12:58.940
Well, imagine a massive accident suddenly completely blocks the main arterial highway.

12:58.940 --> 13:05.200
There is no centralized traffic dictator who individually calls thousands of drivers to assign them a new

13:05.200 --> 13:05.820
route, right?

13:05.820 --> 13:06.080
Right.

13:06.220 --> 13:07.320
Everyone's just reacting.

13:07.820 --> 13:08.280
Exactly.

13:09.000 --> 13:12.220
The drivers themselves organically begin turning down side streets.

13:12.960 --> 13:17.580
The flow of cars redistributes itself around the blockage based on the local constraints

13:17.580 --> 13:18.660
each driver faces.

13:18.880 --> 13:19.080
Uh-huh.

13:19.280 --> 13:23.620
So the city as a whole computes the solution to the traffic jam through the distributed,

13:23.960 --> 13:26.000
decentralized decisions of the localized units.

13:26.000 --> 13:30.400
That is an excellent grounded translation of the concept, and it perfectly sets up the

13:30.400 --> 13:32.300
next major phase of the paper's argument.

13:32.640 --> 13:37.240
Because for that distributed computation to work for the city to successfully reroute traffic

13:37.240 --> 13:41.520
around the highway pileup, you absolutely must have side streets available to turn down.

13:41.880 --> 13:47.940
And if the city paves over all those side streets to make one hyper-efficient superhighway,

13:48.520 --> 13:49.900
what happens when there's an accident?

13:50.640 --> 13:52.480
Doesn't the whole computation just freeze?

13:52.620 --> 13:53.520
It freezes completely.

13:53.520 --> 13:56.840
That seems to be the exact trap our industrial models are falling into.

13:57.640 --> 14:01.700
But to really understand how human activity breaks these systems, we need to look at the

14:01.700 --> 14:02.720
geometry of survival.

14:03.280 --> 14:08.820
The paper draws this really stark difference between excess capacity and over-compression.

14:09.700 --> 14:13.480
This section of the text is where the math really starts to indict our entire economic

14:13.480 --> 14:14.660
and industrial worldview.

14:15.220 --> 14:16.640
It is a profound critique.

14:16.960 --> 14:18.700
Because we hate excess capacity, right?

14:18.700 --> 14:19.100
We do.

14:19.300 --> 14:23.800
For over a century, we have been explicitly taught in business schools, in engineering

14:23.800 --> 14:28.180
programs, and in agricultural science that redundancy is practically a sin.

14:28.820 --> 14:33.540
The industrial mindset looks at a system and says, if every single component isn't operating

14:33.540 --> 14:36.380
at 100% capacity at all times, it is inefficient.

14:36.940 --> 14:38.320
And inefficiency is waste.

14:38.460 --> 14:39.880
And waste must be eliminated.

14:39.880 --> 14:41.100
We see this everywhere.

14:41.260 --> 14:45.500
Like, if a corporation has employees who aren't constantly maximizing their throughput every

14:45.500 --> 14:50.800
minute of the day, a consulting firm comes in and optimizes those employees right out of

14:50.800 --> 14:52.220
a job just to thin out the payroll.

14:52.340 --> 14:52.700
Exactly.

14:53.380 --> 14:57.140
But the paper makes a critical mathematical distinction here.

14:57.680 --> 15:05.380
It separates simple, useless redundancy from a biological concept called functional degeneracy.

15:05.380 --> 15:09.600
Which is such a tricky term, because the word degeneracy usually carries such a negative

15:09.600 --> 15:10.360
connotation.

15:10.740 --> 15:15.600
But here, functional degeneracy is presented as the holy grail of system survival.

15:15.820 --> 15:16.560
It really is.

15:16.600 --> 15:20.700
When I first read the term, I was struggling to differentiate it from just having a backup

15:20.700 --> 15:21.060
plan.

15:21.380 --> 15:23.920
I thought of it like having a spare tire in your trunk.

15:24.460 --> 15:26.500
But a spare tire is just simple redundancy, right?

15:26.520 --> 15:30.700
It's just a carbon copy of the thing you were already using, sitting there doing nothing

15:30.700 --> 15:31.560
until you need it.

15:31.760 --> 15:32.020
Right.

15:32.020 --> 15:36.840
Simple redundancy is having three identical steering wheels in your car.

15:37.720 --> 15:39.220
It is useless duplication.

15:40.040 --> 15:42.260
Functional degeneracy is entirely different.

15:42.700 --> 15:47.180
It means having components or pathways in a system that are structurally distinct-like.

15:47.480 --> 15:49.140
They normally do very different jobs.

15:49.360 --> 15:54.240
But they possess overlapping functional capacities that can be called upon under stress.

15:54.440 --> 15:57.240
Like cross-training in a high-end restaurant kitchen.

15:57.480 --> 15:59.060
Oh, walk me through that scenario.

15:59.060 --> 16:01.900
Okay, so imagine a bustling commercial kitchen.

16:02.020 --> 16:02.340
Yeah.

16:02.440 --> 16:06.480
You have a head chef, a sous chef, a pastry chef, a line cook, and a dishwasher.

16:07.080 --> 16:11.520
Under normal, optimal conditions, they all have completely distinct structural roles.

16:12.040 --> 16:13.600
The pastry chef is making desserts.

16:13.980 --> 16:15.900
The dishwasher is scrubbing plates.

16:16.260 --> 16:16.540
Right.

16:16.620 --> 16:18.000
There is no simple redundancy.

16:18.120 --> 16:21.980
Like, you don't pay three dishwashers just to stand around watching one guy wash plates.

16:22.400 --> 16:26.700
But suddenly, the line cook cuts their hand and has to go to the hospital right in the

16:26.700 --> 16:27.640
middle of the dinner rush.

16:27.740 --> 16:29.120
A sudden shock to the system.

16:29.540 --> 16:29.980
Exactly.

16:30.580 --> 16:35.160
But because this kitchen has functional degeneracy, the sous chef can step down and take over

16:35.160 --> 16:39.920
the grill, the pastry chef can temporarily manage the appetizer station, and even the

16:39.920 --> 16:44.220
dishwasher, who has been watching the line for a year, can step up and plate the salads.

16:44.440 --> 16:45.380
That's perfect.

16:45.440 --> 16:49.800
They aren't perfectly optimized for those roles, and the kitchen might run a little slower.

16:49.800 --> 16:55.800
But the overlapping skill sets allow the system to reroute the workflow and survive the dinner

16:55.800 --> 16:56.220
rush.

16:56.780 --> 16:59.220
That is a phenomenal mapping of the concept.

16:59.800 --> 17:02.800
They are structurally distinct, but functionally overlapping.

17:03.300 --> 17:08.580
In a natural ecosystem, functional degeneracy might look like having several different species of

17:08.580 --> 17:09.560
insect pollinators.

17:10.240 --> 17:14.460
Normally, they each prefer entirely different types of primary flowers.

17:14.460 --> 17:20.160
But if a disease wipes out the main pollinator for a critical plant species, these other

17:20.160 --> 17:25.660
insects have the anatomical capacity, that overlapping function, to shift their behavior and pollinate

17:25.660 --> 17:28.780
that plant, saving the broader ecosystem from collapse.

17:28.980 --> 17:31.020
Here's where it gets really interesting, though.

17:31.500 --> 17:36.160
The paper maps this out mathematically, essentially showing an equation where the dimension of your

17:36.160 --> 17:38.120
future options physically expands.

17:38.120 --> 17:43.300
Yes, the text notes that functional degeneracy increases the dimensionality of the admissible

17:43.300 --> 17:44.560
future trajectories.

17:44.720 --> 17:45.880
Dimensionality of the futures.

17:46.100 --> 17:51.420
