21:12.540 --> 21:21.540
And understanding why it doesn't leads us into another major paradigm shift in Flicksheen's paper, the concept of topology as memory.

21:21.740 --> 21:23.100
Topology as memory, okay.

21:23.320 --> 21:26.160
The framework the author introduces here is called sphere pop.

21:26.580 --> 21:32.960
It is a direct challenge to classical network theory, which often treats networks as if they only exist in the present static moment.

21:33.460 --> 21:36.080
But complex adaptive systems accumulate history.

21:36.080 --> 21:39.220
They remember, but obviously not with a centralized brain.

21:39.780 --> 21:41.460
Like a forest doesn't have a hippocampus.

21:41.460 --> 21:44.700
So how does a dispersed ecosystem remember a drought?

21:44.800 --> 21:50.220
It remembers through its topology the physical architecture of how the nodes and pathways are connected to one another.

21:50.840 --> 21:53.660
The paper introduces the concept of the admissibility log.

21:53.720 --> 21:54.640
The admissibility log.

21:54.640 --> 21:59.060
Now, this is not a literal physical ledger hidden in the soil.

21:59.920 --> 22:06.540
It is a mathematical representation of the irreversible accumulation of successful constraint updates.

22:06.540 --> 22:16.160
Meaning that every single time the ecosystem is forced to rewire to survive a crisis, it permanently alters its foundational structure for the future.

22:16.420 --> 22:17.900
Consider the wolf and the rabbit again.

22:18.460 --> 22:23.780
If a severe drought wipes out the deer, the wolf shifts its hunting vectors to the rabbit population.

22:23.780 --> 22:33.160
Over a few years, this might alter the vegetation the rabbits normally eat, which in turn alters the soil composition, which encourages a different type of grass to grow.

22:33.160 --> 22:40.800
When the drought finally ends and the deer slowly return, the ecosystem does not just magically snap back to its pre-drought state.

22:41.180 --> 22:42.160
The soil is different.

22:42.440 --> 22:43.520
The underbrush is different.

22:43.880 --> 22:45.680
The wolf has developed new hunting patterns.

22:46.200 --> 22:49.980
The system has inscribed its own adaptive history into its physical geometry.

22:50.740 --> 22:53.880
Topology becomes a distributed physical memory substrate.

22:54.320 --> 22:55.840
It makes me think of folding a piece of paper.

22:55.900 --> 22:56.500
Oh, how so?

22:56.500 --> 23:05.420
Well, if you take a pristine, perfectly flat piece of paper and you fold it sharply in half to jam it into your pocket, you change its structure.

23:06.100 --> 23:08.440
Later, you take it out and you try to unfold it.

23:08.680 --> 23:10.020
You can press it down on a table.

23:10.480 --> 23:14.300
You can smooth it out with your hands and you can try to make it look exactly the way it did before.

23:15.300 --> 23:17.760
But the crease is permanently there.

23:17.900 --> 23:18.260
Yes.

23:18.580 --> 23:24.060
The actual physical fibers of the paper have been fundamentally broken and realigned.

23:24.060 --> 23:31.360
If you try to bend that piece of paper again in the future, it is going to naturally want to fold along that historical crease.

23:31.800 --> 23:35.640
The paper physically remembers the stress it was put under.

23:35.860 --> 23:39.420
The physical reality of the ecosystem is exactly like that crease.

23:39.660 --> 23:42.220
The mathematical term for this is non-commutative.

23:42.640 --> 23:48.580
In mathematics, an operation is commutative if the order doesn't matter, like 2 plus 3 is the same as 3 plus 2.

23:48.760 --> 23:48.940
Right.

23:49.160 --> 23:51.440
But ecological history is non-commutative.

23:51.440 --> 23:55.740
The order of events fundamentally changes the final geometry.

23:56.620 --> 24:11.440
If a forest experiences a massive fire and then 10 years later experiences an invasive beetle infestation, the resulting ecosystem will look wildly different structurally than if the beetle infestation had happened first, followed by the fire 10 years later.

24:11.700 --> 24:12.040
Wow.

24:12.180 --> 24:13.280
So the sequence matters.

24:13.560 --> 24:14.020
Completely.

24:14.020 --> 24:17.660
The system carries the imprint of the sequence of its adaptations.

24:18.000 --> 24:27.100
But if nature is always moving forward, always accumulating these permanent topological creases, doesn't that completely invalidate the way we approach environmental restoration?

24:27.340 --> 24:29.200
