37:02.020 --> 37:05.700
And the paper applies this exact same logic to artificial intelligence,

37:05.700 --> 37:10.660
specifically discussing the dangers of machine learning models that lack excess capacity.

37:10.660 --> 37:15.060
I want to explore this because AI is just completely dominating our current reality.

37:15.520 --> 37:18.860
What does it mean for an AI to have excess representational capacity?

37:18.860 --> 37:25.280
When computer scientists train massive AI models, there is a constant tension regarding efficiency.

37:25.820 --> 37:30.800
If they compress a model too much, meaning they force the neural network to learn the absolute

37:30.800 --> 37:36.380
bare minimum mathematical pathways needed to get the right answer on its specific training data,

37:37.100 --> 37:39.200
the model becomes incredibly brittle.

37:39.500 --> 37:40.560
It becomes over-compressed.

37:40.560 --> 37:40.960
Yes.

37:41.340 --> 37:47.400
The moment you deploy that AI into the real world and feed it data that looks even slightly different

37:47.400 --> 37:52.080
from what it trained on, a scenario the text refers to as a distributional shift,

37:52.620 --> 37:54.740
the AI fails catastrophically.

37:54.960 --> 37:59.380
It makes bizarre hallucinatory errors because it has no geometric flexibility.

37:59.600 --> 38:02.480
It's like a student who wants to be perfectly efficient with their study time.

38:02.880 --> 38:05.080
They get a copy of last year's standardized test,

38:05.400 --> 38:08.480
and instead of learning the underlying concepts of algebra or history,

38:08.480 --> 38:13.180
they just memorize the exact sequence of multiple choice answers, A, C, D, D.

38:13.300 --> 38:13.480
Right.

38:13.620 --> 38:14.600
They optimize the output.

38:14.960 --> 38:16.320
They have optimized their studying.

38:16.700 --> 38:19.700
They have absolutely zero excess cognitive capacity.

38:20.580 --> 38:25.920
But the moment they sit down for the real exam and the teacher changes the wording of a single

38:25.920 --> 38:30.740
question or shifts the order of the answers, the student completely and utterly fails.

38:31.220 --> 38:34.800
They didn't learn the messy, redundant, underlying concepts.

38:35.180 --> 38:37.200
They lacked representational richness.

38:37.200 --> 38:41.480
That is a brilliant translation of the AI training problem.

38:42.020 --> 38:46.980
Models built with massive excess representational capacity models that are allowed to learn

38:46.980 --> 38:51.800
overlapping, redundant, high-dimensional patterns are the only ones that can generalize.

38:51.860 --> 38:52.900
They can handle the unexpected.

38:53.240 --> 38:56.900
They can survive the distributional shift of the real world because they have

38:56.900 --> 39:00.180
multiple mathematical pathways to arrive at a logical conclusion.

39:00.540 --> 39:04.640
And we see this mirrored in our economic and technological infrastructures as well.

39:04.640 --> 39:07.960
You mean like decentralized supply chains or even internet protocols?

39:08.560 --> 39:08.820
Yes.

39:09.360 --> 39:13.540
Highly centralized, perfectly synchronized communication protocols or corporate supply

39:13.540 --> 39:18.100
chains are incredibly fast and profitable under stable normal conditions.

39:18.500 --> 39:24.440
But a decentralized, partially redundant system, which looks slower, messier, and vastly more

39:24.440 --> 39:29.260
expensive to a corporate accountant, is the one that actually survives geopolitical disruptions,

39:29.560 --> 39:31.920
server outages, or natural disasters.

39:31.920 --> 39:35.440
It survives because it has alternative pathways.

39:35.940 --> 39:42.100
So if we synthesize all of this across ecology, neuroscience, and economics, a robust system resists

39:42.100 --> 39:42.660
change.

39:43.040 --> 39:45.520
A resilient system eventually bounces back from change.

39:45.960 --> 39:51.260
But a transformable system, which is what Kluxin argues we must build, survives by actively

39:51.260 --> 39:54.460
and continuously reorganizing its own fundamental structure.

39:54.460 --> 39:58.880
And this leads to the paper's most profound and perhaps most alarming conclusion.

39:59.360 --> 40:03.300
The author argues that the Anthropocene is not merely a crisis of carbon emissions or plastic

40:03.300 --> 40:03.700
pollution.

40:04.240 --> 40:08.460
It is a large-scale admissibility compression across planetary systems.

40:08.620 --> 40:09.620
That's a terrifying phrase.

40:09.880 --> 40:15.440
We are systematically optimizing ourselves into a corner across global finance, technological

40:15.440 --> 40:19.840
infrastructure, and planetary agriculture all at the exact same time.

40:19.840 --> 40:24.260
We are aggressively hunting down and destroying the excess capacity of the entire planet.

40:24.760 --> 40:30.600
Does this mean that the ultimate, inescapable law of the universe is that efficiency is

40:30.600 --> 40:32.200
the literal opposite of survival?

40:32.600 --> 40:37.820
If we want our businesses, our brains, and our societies to endure the coming decades, do

40:37.820 --> 40:41.080
we actively, purposefully need to build messiness into them?

40:41.300 --> 40:43.760
The mathematics of this paper would say yes, absolutely.

40:44.180 --> 40:46.880
We have to abandon the illusion of perfect optimization.

40:46.880 --> 40:52.540
Stable systems are not the ones that aggressively minimize their unused possibility space to

40:52.540 --> 40:53.800
maximize short-term profit.

40:54.120 --> 40:54.180
Right.

40:54.540 --> 40:59.120
Truly stable systems are the ones that intentionally preserve enough overlapping possibility space

40:59.120 --> 41:02.260
to survive continuous, unpredictable transformation.

41:02.880 --> 41:07.700
The messiness, the redundancy, the overlapping inefficiencies, the unpaved side streets, that is

41:07.700 --> 41:08.360
not waste.

41:08.500 --> 41:10.520
That is the very architecture of survival.

41:10.740 --> 41:12.140
That is incredibly profound.

41:12.140 --> 41:19.560
To wrap this deep dive up, the overarching thesis of The Geometry of Rewiring is that survival

41:19.560 --> 41:22.420
in a chaotic universe is not about standing your ground.

41:22.840 --> 41:25.740
It is never about defending the delicate house of cards.

41:26.160 --> 41:26.880
No, it's not.

41:26.960 --> 41:27.320
True.

41:27.800 --> 41:31.260
Lasting stability is about maintaining the navigability of your future.

41:31.700 --> 41:37.060
It is about making absolutely sure you always have a side street to turn down when the

41:37.060 --> 41:38.800
main highway inevitably collapses.

41:38.800 --> 41:44.020
It requires a radical inversion of everything the Industrial Revolution taught us.

41:44.420 --> 41:48.940
We must stop trying to freeze life into a static equilibrium, and we must start aggressively

41:48.940 --> 41:53.040
protecting the physical and geometric spaces that allow life to become something entirely

41:53.040 --> 41:53.420
new.

41:53.640 --> 41:58.080
I want to leave you, the listener, with a thought to mull over today as you go about your routine.

41:58.480 --> 42:03.880
Think about your own life, your career trajectory, your daily habits, your deeply held personal

42:03.880 --> 42:05.740
beliefs, even your financial planning.

42:05.740 --> 42:10.980
Have you optimized your life so tightly and made yourself so hyper-efficient that you

42:10.980 --> 42:13.280
have absolutely no excess capacity left?

42:13.520 --> 42:15.260
It's a tough question to ask yourself.

42:15.520 --> 42:16.200
It really is.

42:16.640 --> 42:21.660
If a sudden, unpredictable shock hits your system tomorrow, a sudden job loss, a massive

42:21.660 --> 42:27.340
shift in your industry, a global disruption, or a personal crisis, do you possess the admissible

42:27.340 --> 42:29.460
trajectories to rewire yourself?

42:29.980 --> 42:33.980
Do you have the overlapping skills and the mental flexibility to change your structure?

42:33.980 --> 42:39.220
Or, have you trapped yourself in a beautiful, highly profitable, but ultimately brittle house

42:39.220 --> 42:39.740
of cards?

42:40.240 --> 42:43.900
Maybe the ultimate goal of life isn't to survive exactly as you are right now.

42:44.140 --> 42:47.720
Maybe the goal is to make sure you always have enough options to become something else

42:47.720 --> 42:49.720
entirely.

